  "worked_solution": "The points of inflection are the points where\n\n- the function's concavity changes sign, and\n\n- the tangent line to the graph exists.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our graph.\n\n- The point $x=0$ is *not* a point of inflection. The function does not change concavity. It's concave down both to the left and to the right of $x=0.$\n\n- The point $x=1$ is *not* a point of inflection. Although the graph changes concavity from concave down to concave up at $x=1,$  the function is not defined at this point, so the tangent line does not exist either.\n\n- The point $x=2$ is not a point of inflection. The function does not change concavity. It's concave up both to the left and to the right of $x=2.$\n\nTherefore, there are no points of inflection.",
